L421: Nominees\nL422: \nL423: The Eternal Memory\nL424: \nL425: Maite Alberdi\nL426: \nL427: Nominees\nL428: \nL429: Four Daughters\nL430: \nL431: Kaouther Ben Hania and Nadim Cheikhrouha\nL432: \nL433: Nominees\nL434: \nL435: To Kill a Tiger\nL436: \nL437: Nisha Pahuja, Cornelia Principe and David Oppenheim\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn13view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.guinnessworldrecords.jp/world-records/72369-highest-grossing-japanese-animated-film-at-the-global-box-office\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.guinnessworldrecords.jp/world-records/72369-highest-grossing-japanese-animated-film-at-the-global-box-office is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Dull Sword [the longest, digitally restored version] | Details of the work | Japanese Animated Film Classics (https://animation.filmarchives.jp/en/works/view/100183)\nciteturn13view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://animation.filmarchives.jp/en/works/view/100183\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 86\nL0: Top of page\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Japanese Animated Film Classics】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Japanese Animated Film Classics】\nL5: \nL6:   * 【2†Notice(backnumber)】\nL7:   * 【3†FAQ】\nL8:   * 【4†JAPANESE】\nL9: Toggle navigation MENU\nL10: # The Dull Sword [the longest, digitally restored version]\nL11: \nL12: 【5†Watch】\nL13: Original Title  | なまくら刀[デジタル復元・最長版][白黒ポジ染色版]\nL14: Japanese kana Rendering  | Namakuragatana\nL15: English Title  | The Dull Sword [the longest, digitally restored version]\nL16: Production Date  | 1917\nL17: Author  | 【6†Junichi Kouchi】\nL18: Duration (minutes)  | 4\nL19: Sound  | silent\nL20: Color  | b/w, tinted\nL21: Plot  |\nL22: Description  | The following three works are said to be the forerunners of Japanese animation films: Hekoten Shimokawa's Mukuzo Imokawa - Genkanban no Maki (芋川椋三玄関番の巻), which was released in a series in January, May, and June 1917; Monkey and the Crabs (猿蟹合戦) by Seitaro Kitayama, and The Dull Sword (a.k.a. Hanawa Hekonai - The Famous Sword) (なまくら刀) by Junichi Kouchi.\nL23: Of these three, the only existing film was a two-minute version of The Dull Sword, which was digitally restored in 2008 with the cooperation of Natsuki Matsumoto, a visual culture historian, from a tinted nitrate positive (yellow) owned by Matsumoto. This version (publicly presented at Cinema: Lost and Found 2008) had been believed to be nearly the same as the original version.\nL24: However, the research described below revealed that it is mostly the second half of the original, while a newly discovered [2014] tinted nitrate positive (green) is composed mostly of its first half.\nL25: The most important factor that motivates film restoration at NFC is the discovery of a work not stored here yet that played an important role in film history. The tinted nitrate positive (green) of The Dull Sword was included in the Nankoin Collection, which was officially donated to NFC in FY2008.\nL26: The footage was inserted between a fragment of an animation in which a line is drawn gradually to complete a pot and a fragment of a live-action film of a man trick riding a bicycle (the titles of the two films are unknown). The part omitted in the previously restored version was hidden in the NFC collection.\nL27: The first issue we encountered when reconstructing The Dull Sword (longest digitally restored version) was figuring out which and how many frames to choose from the previously restored version (yellow) and the newly discovered tinted nitrate positive (green) as source material for restoration. The two versions often complement each other, frame by frame.\nL28: Consequently, creating a fully restored version might cause the color of the animation to change alternately between green and yellow within a brief single cut, which could be confusing to the audience. Fortunately, however, only several frames were missing consecutively from the two sources.\nL29: Therefore, judging that it was possible to restore a nearly perfect version, we adopted the policy of using all 3,180 of the newly discovered frames and making up for missing parts by using the 810 frames found in the previously restored version.\nL30: More specifically, we decided to use frames from the previously restored version in the following three parts of the film: (1) the main title \"The Dull Sword\"; (2) the intertitle \"Let's try out the sword,\" which was turned clockwise by 90 degrees; and (3) from the scene where the samurai (Hanawa Hekonai), attacking a blind person from behind with a sword, is counterattacked, until the end.\nL31: The two sources (one tinted in yellow and the other in green) differ in color as well as in tone and quality. Therefore, the next issue was how to mitigate the difference between them by digital restoration and reduce incongruities in appearance. Parts of the tinted nitrate positive (green) close to the core were already beginning to melt due to deterioration caused by aging.\nL32: We, however, adopted the policy of using as much of the newly discovered version as possible, and of avoiding forcibly adjusting the two sources' image tones and qualities that were difficult to unify, including the part shifting to a scene ((3) above) in the previously restored version. For that reason, we decided to leave most of the respective characteristics of the two materials, keeping digital alteration to a minimum.\nL33: Eventually, we adopted the following workflow for restoration: For the three parts that require the previously restored version, we used the digitally restored data (Digital Source Master, DSM) created in 2008. For the first half that requires the newly discovered version, we prepared a black-and-white internegative and then scanned it at a 2K resolution to digitally remove dirt and scratches, stabilize it and eliminate flickering etc.\nL34: We then conformed (edited) these two sets of digital data and created a new DSM after grading (color adjustment) to record it into a black-and-white negative.\n"
